    {
      "number": 2,
      "title": "Le rayon des tentations",
      "period": "Saturday, 10 a.m., fruit and vegetable aisles, end-of-aisle displays and the freezer section",
      "summary": "Gaston tries to follow his list through the fruit and vegetable aisles, but tempting displays and a flurry of promotions derail him. Madame Roux teaches him to compare unit prices and use her coupons while he weighs fruit on the scales and is lured into a bulk lot of things he doesn't need. The trolley wheel squeaks louder with every impulse buy.",
      "key_facts": [
        {
          "claim": "In French supermarkets, loose fruit and vegetables are often weighed by the customer on a self-service scale that prints a price label with a barcode."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Shelf labels in France must show the price per kilo or per litre (prix au kilo), which lets shoppers compare products of different sizes."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Own-brand products (marques de distributeur, like Marque Repère or Carrefour's own range) are usually cheaper than national brands."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Supermarkets place special offers and loss leaders on the end-of-aisle displays (têtes de gondole) to encourage impulse buying."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Loyalty cards (cartes de fidélité) give French shoppers points, discount vouchers (bons de réduction) or personalised offers."
        },
        {
          "claim": "The frozen food section is called les surgelés, and products must keep a use-by date (date limite de consommation) for fresh food, or a best-before date (date de durabilité minimale) for dry goods."
        }
      ],
