    {
      "number": 3,
      "title": "Boucherie, boulangerie et compagnie",
      "period": "Saturday, 10:45 a.m., the butcher's counter, bakery and deli counter inside the supermarket",
      "summary": "Gaston queues at the butcher's counter for a chicken and fish while Kévin restocks and announces that everything is out of stock, one item after another. A baguette is still warm at the bakery, but the deli counter has a queue of hesitating customers, and a jostle at the tray of cakes leaves Gaston apologising to a stranger. Tins, jars, bottles and packets pile up in the trolley.",
      "key_facts": [
        {
          "claim": "Many French supermarkets have a staffed butcher's counter (boucherie), a bakery (boulangerie) and a deli (rayon traiteur) where you take a ticket from a machine and wait your turn."
        },
        {
          "claim": "A traditional baguette is typically sold for around one euro in France, and by law a baguette de tradition has no additives."
        },
        {
          "claim": "A dozen eggs is called une douzaine d'œufs, and in France eggs are usually sold unrefrigerated on shelves, with a number code on each egg."
        },
        {
          "claim": "All packaged food in France shows a date limite de consommation (DLC, 'à consommer jusqu'au') for perishable products or a date de durabilité minimale (DDM, 'à consommer de préférence avant') for others."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Typical staples in a French shopping trolley include bread, butter, cheese, yoghurt, ham, pasta, rice, flour, sugar, biscuits and wine."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Fruit juice, wine and water are often sold in multipacks (lots) such as packs of six bottles of water."
        }
      ],
