    {
      "number": 4,
      "title": "La queue interminable",
      "period": "Saturday, 11:30 a.m., the checkout area of the crowded supermarket",
      "summary": "With a full trolley, Gaston faces an endless queue. He tries the self-checkout, which keeps declaring an 'unexpected item in the bagging area', then switches to a regular checkout where a woman in a hurry slips in front of him. Josiane stays calm while an item goes missing from the bag and the queue jostles around.",
      "key_facts": [
        {
          "claim": "French supermarkets have staffed checkouts, self-checkouts (caisses automatiques) and often a priority checkout (caisse prioritaire) for pregnant women, people with disabilities and elderly people."
        },
        {
          "claim": "On French supermarket conveyor belts, customers use a small plastic divider bar to separate their shopping from the next person's."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Self-checkouts in France say 'Veuillez patienter, un instant' and 'Il y a une anomalie dans le sac' when the weight of the bag is different from what is expected."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Cashiers in France say 'Bonjour' to every customer and 'Merci, bonne journée' at the end, and customers are expected to greet them back."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Security guards (agents de sécurité) may be posted near the exit of large French supermarkets and can ask to see receipts or bags."
        }
