    {
      "number": 5,
      "title": "Le coup de bambou",
      "period": "Saturday, noon, at the checkout till and the exit",
      "summary": "At the checkout, Gaston discovers the total is far higher than his budget, and his bank card is declined because he mistypes the PIN, then the contactless payment limit blocks him. In panic he tries to pay in cash but doesn't have enough euros, and the wheel finally jams the trolley and sends it skidding into a display of tins, which topple in the comic climax. Josiane, the manager and a security guard arrive.",
      "key_facts": [
        {
          "claim": "In France, contactless card payments (paiement sans contact) are limited to a single transaction of 50 euros, above which you have to enter your PIN (code confidentiel)."
        },
        {
          "claim": "French bank cards (carte bancaire) use a four-digit PIN and most card readers (terminaux de paiement) ask you to enter it after a few contactless payments in a row."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Tipping is not expected at French supermarkets, and a service charge is already included in prices in cafés and restaurants (service compris)."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Shoppers keep their receipt (ticket de caisse) as proof of purchase to get refunds or exchanges (remboursement or échange)."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Cash (espèces) is still accepted at French supermarket checkouts, although cashiers may ask if you have the exact change (l'appoint)."
        },
        {
          "claim": "The euro is divided into 100 cents, and banknotes come in denominations of 5, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200 and 500 euros."
        }
      ],
