    {
      "number": 6,
      "title": "Le dîner de la dernière chance",
      "period": "Saturday evening, Gaston's flat and kitchen in Tours",
      "summary": "The manager forgives the tinned avalanche and gives Gaston a refund and a discount voucher, and Madame Roux helps him carry the heavy bags home. In the kitchen, they cook a rescue dinner from the improbable shopping, with the squeaking trolley wheel as a funny souvenir. Everything ends warmly, with the neighbour at the table and a promise to shop together next Saturday.",
      "key_facts": [
        {
          "claim": "French dinners traditionally include several courses: an entrée (starter), a plat (main course), cheese, and a dessert."
        },
        {
          "claim": "In France, the cheese course comes before dessert and is often served with bread rather than with crackers."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Typical French supermarkets sell fresh produce, packaged food, drinks, household items and often have in-store loyalty programmes."
        },
        {
          "claim": "The French often say 'bon appétit' before eating and 'à votre santé' or 'santé !' when raising a glass."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Supermarkets are required to reduce food waste: since 2016 French law forbids large supermarkets from destroying unsold edible food, and they must donate it to charities."
        }
